---
import LessonLayout from '../../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
---
<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; The Liturgy: Encounter with Christ" cic="CCC 1066-1134" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in a group. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What does the word <em>&laquo;liturgy&raquo;</em> mean and where does it come from?</li>
<li>Who is the true actor in the liturgy: the priest, the assembly, or Christ?</li>
<li>What is the <em>&laquo;Paschal Mystery&raquo;</em> and why is it the heart of the liturgy?</li>
<li>What is the difference between the common priesthood of the faithful and the ministerial priesthood?</li>
<li>Name at least three signs or symbols used in the liturgy.</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly. There are no <em>&laquo;correct&raquo;</em> answers to these personal questions.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. When was the last time you felt that Mass was truly an <em>&laquo;encounter with Christ&raquo;</em>?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Describe the experience: where was it, what happened, how did you feel?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Is there something about the liturgy that you find difficult to understand or that distracts you?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">What could you do to overcome that difficulty?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. Do you live Mass as a spectator or as a protagonist?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">What would you change to participate more actively in upcoming Masses?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. How can you bring the liturgy into your daily life outside the Church?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Mass ends with <em>&laquo;Go in peace.&raquo;</em> It is a mission, not a <em>&laquo;see you later.&raquo;</em> How do you live that mission?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. If the liturgy is Christ's work and not ours, does that free you from the pressure that <em>&laquo;everything must be perfect&raquo;</em>?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">How does this change your attitude toward participating in Mass?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. Read Luke 24:13-35 (Emmaus) aloud</h4>
<p>After reading, write which part of the story you identify with most. Are you the sad disciple, the one who listens to the explanation, or the one who recognizes Jesus in the breaking of the bread?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>B. Attend Mass with a special focus</h4>
<p>At the next Mass you attend, choose one specific moment to pay close attention: the readings, the consecration, the Our Father, Communion. After Mass, write what you discovered.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>C. Research the Liturgical Year</h4>
<p>Look up the liturgical seasons in the Catechism (CCC 1163-1173) or online. Make a simple calendar marking the season we are in now and the liturgical colors of each season.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Memory Verse</h3>

<div class="bible-passage">
<p><em>&laquo;They devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching and to fellowship, to the breaking of bread and to prayer.&raquo;</em></p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">&mdash; Acts 2:42</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Prayer for This Week</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord Jesus, who walked to Emmaus with the disciples<br />
and opened their eyes in the breaking of the bread,<br />
walk with me in every Mass,<br />
open my eyes to recognize you,<br />
set my heart on fire with your Word.<br />
May I never grow accustomed to the liturgy,<br />
but may each celebration be a living encounter with you.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

